<%# ONE ACCOUNT, AND NOTHING INSIDE IT (two-ledger spec §2). See the UI design spec §4.3.
    Locals: `presenter` (HomePresenter), `account` (a Pool of type account).

    THE POOLS NESTED HERE ARE GONE (Task 6). An account used to hold its envelopes, so this section
    rendered `presenter.pools_for(account)` beneath the header; a category lives nowhere and holds
    its own money, so an account has exactly one thing to say — what the bank says — and the
    categories band above says the rest.

    The header carries `balance_of`, which for MAIN is the pot. It was `current_buffer_for`, and the
    figure moved with the model: that reader answered an account's cash NET of the envelopes housed
    in it, and there is no such remainder now. What the distribute screen calls AVAILABLE is the
    money no category has claimed, on the other ledger entirely, so one noun over the two quantities
    would have been a lie on both. (The noun itself is retired app-wide — answers-first Home §3.)

    THE ` · target $2,000.00` CLAUSE IS DELETED (Task 7). It printed `pools.target-amount`, a
    marker for the cash an account was meant to keep spare, and the concept goes with the pool
    screens: two-ledger §2 gives accounts no target semantics — money with no job is FREE, and
    computed claims makes that a derived figure (`ClaimLedger#free`) rather than anything an
    account could aim at — the last reader of it was deleted with the distribution, the plan's T8
    drops the column, and the account form that set it is now name-only. A number nothing can
    change and nothing reads is worse than no number.

    RENAME AND DELETE LIVE HERE NOW, because Home IS the accounts index: `pools/index` and
    `pools/show` carried those two buttons and both screens are deleted. %>
<% balance = presenter.balance_of(account) %>
<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" data-account-group="<%= account.name %>">
  <%# ONE HEADER ROW, NOT TWO (design review H9). The name and the balance sat in one strip and
      Rename/Delete in a second one below it, which on a funded account with nothing else to say
      rendered as an empty band of white between two borders — a card whose BODY was a pair of
      links. The two doors are housekeeping, so they belong beside the name, not under the money.

      IT STACKS BELOW `sm`: on a 375px screen the name, the balance and two controls cannot share a
      line, and the balance is the figure that must not be the thing that wraps. %>
  <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-200 flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline sm:justify-between gap-2 sm:gap-4">
    <%# `flex-wrap` AND NO `truncate` (measured at 375px). Truncating the name put "Ev…" beside a
        full-length "Main account — everything flows through it": the app abbreviated the one word
        the user typed in order to keep a sentence it wrote itself. The note wraps to its own line
        below `sm` instead, and the name is never shortened. %>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1 min-w-0">
      <%# Not the `uppercase` eyebrow this codebase gives short labels like "Current Balance": an
          account name is a proper noun the user typed, and rendering "Ally Savings" as ALLY SAVINGS
          shouts a name back at them. %>
      <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900"><%= account.name %></h3>
      <%# ** MAIN HAS NO DELETE BUTTON (final fix wave, C-1). ** `Pool#main_account_is_not_deletable`
        is the refusal and this is the affordance agreeing with it: main is on one side of every
        AccountMovement the app writes, so destroying it would take the whole physical ledger with
        it — the model halts, and a button that always ends in a red flash is worse than no button.

        THE LABEL RATHER THAN A DISABLED BUTTON, because the absence is a fact about the account
        rather than a state it might leave: main is main until the user changes it, and changing it
        is a future affordance this app does not have yet. The screen says which account this is,
        which is also the only place Home names the role at all. %>
      <% if presenter.main?(account) %>
        <span class="text-xs font-medium text-gray-500" data-account-main="<%= account.name %>">
          Main account — everything flows through it
        </span>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <div class="flex items-baseline gap-4 shrink-0">
      <%# An overdraft is the loudest thing in the app and this is the row that owns the number, so
          it says so here too rather than leaving the standing band's sentence as the only place a
          negative balance is visible. %>
      <span class="text-sm <%= balance.negative? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-700" %>">
        balance now <strong><%= number_to_currency(balance) %></strong>
      </span>

      <%# THE TWO DOORS AN ACCOUNT STILL HAS — and MAIN has only one of them. Deliberately quiet —
          this screen is about money, and a rename is housekeeping — but present, because deleting
          them with the Pools screens would have left a user no way to correct a typo in a bank name.

          RENAME IS `text-brand`, WHICH IS NOW A READABLE OLIVE (design review H9/B2). It measured
          1.75:1 against this white card while it painted `--color-primary`; the token now resolves
          to #63612F, 6.4:1, with the hover one step darker again.

          DELETE IS TERTIARY UNTIL YOU REACH FOR IT. It was permanently red — the loudest colour in
          the app, spent on the rarest and most destructive act, sitting at the same weight as a
          rename on every account on the page. Grey at rest, red on hover and on focus, which is
          where the warning is actually wanted. %>
      <%= link_to "Rename",
                  edit_bank_account_path(account),
                  class: "text-xs font-medium text-brand hover:text-brand-dark",
                  data: { account_edit: account.name } %>

      <% unless presenter.main?(account) %>
      <%# ** THE CONFIRM SAYS WHAT DELETING AN ACCOUNT ACTUALLY DOES (fix round 1, MED-1; narrowed by
          Task 8; re-verified in the final fix wave). ** Its first draft carried the ENVELOPE sentence
          off the deleted `PoolsHelper#pool_delete_confirmation` — "its movements and any categories
          pointing at it move to your main account" — and every clause of it was false of an account.

          ONE FACT NOW, WHERE THERE WERE TWO, because the second stopped being true when the pool
          layer went: no category points at an account any more (`categories.pool_id` is dropped), so
          the "the delete is refused instead" clause described a refusal nothing can raise. What is
          left is the whole of what happens: `dependent: :destroy` on `movements_in`/`movements_out`
          deletes the account's transfers.

          "GOES BACK TO YOUR MAIN ACCOUNT" IS TRUE, CHECKED AGAINST THE WRITERS RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
          Both of them — `Entry#route_income_to!` and `AccountOpening#write_movement` — put
          `user.default_account` on one end, so main is on the OTHER side of every movement
          this account has. Deleting them subtracts nothing from main and restores each `moves out`
          it was carrying: the pot rises by exactly what this account held, and `pot + Σ accounts` is
          unchanged to the penny. The sentence is only ever rendered here, on a NON-main account,
          which is precisely the population it is true of. %>
        <%# ** AND WHAT IT WAS OPENED WITH IS THE SECOND CLAUSE (fix round round 2 — item 4). ** The
            first sentence was the whole truth while an account could only be funded by a transfer
            FROM main: `dependent: :destroy` takes the movements, main is on the other end of every
            one, so the money comes back. An account that SAID what it holds also carries an opening
            entry (`Pool has_one :opening_entry`), and that money never was main's — the opening's
            entry and its transfer cancel on main exactly — so it leaves the ledger rather than
            returning. Both halves are named here because a user deleting a $500 savings account
            they opened at $500 would otherwise be promised $500 back in checking and get none.
            `BankAccountsController#destroy` measures the pot and says the real figure afterwards. %>
        <% confirm = "Delete #{account.name}? The movements into and out of it are deleted with it, " \
                     "so the money they moved goes back to your main account — and whatever it was " \
                     "opened with goes with it. Your balances will change. This action cannot be " \
                     "undone." %>
        <%= button_to "Delete",
                      bank_account_path(account),
                      method: :delete,
                      class: "text-xs font-medium text-gray-500 hover:text-status-danger " \
                             "focus-visible:text-status-danger transition-colors",
                      form: { class: "inline-block leading-none", data: { turbo_confirm: confirm } },
                      data: { account_delete: account.name } %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>

  <%# ** EDIT BALANCE (account-openings spec §3): the account says what it holds again. ** The two
      onboarding cards that used to render here are DELETED — `_fund_account` (step 2's movement from
      main) and `_opening_balance` (step 3's one-time main correction) — and this is what replaced
      them BOTH: one door, on every account, open forever rather than latched after one use.

      IT REWRITES THE ACCOUNT'S OPENING RECORD RATHER THAN ADDING TO IT (§2, Henry: "Corrections
      should be done on the same initial entry"), and the field opens on TODAY'S balance because that
      is the figure the user is being asked to confirm or correct — `AccountOpening` subtracts
      everything that has flowed through since, so what they type is what this card shows afterwards.

      `<details>`/`<summary>` RATHER THAN A STIMULUS CONTROLLER — the house idiom, stated where the
      same choice was made in `_accounts_line.html.erb` and `budget_page/_suggestions.html.erb`: the
      whole interaction is "show me the form", it needs no JavaScript and it survives a Turbo
      navigation. The link IS the summary.

      A CARD ONLY RENDERS HERE ONCE ITS ACCOUNT HAS AN OPENING RECORD (`#collapsed_accounts`), so
      this is always a correction and never a first answer — the first answer is a row in the "Your
      accounts" card, which is the same form. %>
  <details class="border-t border-gray-200" data-edit-balance="<%= account.name %>">
    <summary class="px-6 py-3 text-xs font-medium text-brand hover:text-brand-dark cursor-pointer">
      Edit balance
    </summary>
    <%= render "opening_row",
        account: account,
        name: nil,
        url: bank_account_opening_path(account),
        method: :patch,
        value: presenter.balance_field_for(account),
        data: { account_balance_form: account.name } %>
  </details>
</section>
